# Build artifacts. `make build` writes bin/<name>; `go build ./...` drops the
# binary at the repo root, which is how bin/define and ./define first got
# committed. Both paths are ignored so neither recurs.
bin/
/define
.DS_Store

/AGENTS.md
/CLAUDE.md
/GEMINI.md
/.claude/skills/
/.agents/skills/
/.claude/settings.json
/.colima/
/construct/scripts/vm-log.sh
/construct/generated/

# define writes its deck to the CURRENT directory.
#
# UN-ANCHORED, and that is the fix rather than the default. The anchored form
# only covered the repo root, while `go test` runs with cwd set to the PACKAGE
# directory and the pty conformance suite launches bin/define from there — so a
# deck appeared at cmd/define/words/ and cmd/define/events/, which no pattern
# matched, and `git add -A` committed the developer's own vocabulary.
#
# That is the same fact — go test runs in the package directory — that made the
# first version of TestNoCommittedBinaries unable to fail. Third time this class
# has cost a review round; TestNoTrackedRuntimeState now covers it.
# The list is single-sourced in store.RuntimeDirs and TestGitignoreCoversRuntimeDirs
# asserts these stay in step — #9 added usage/ and reached none of the three
# places that needed it.
words/
events/
usage/

# `go build ./` inside a cmd/<name>/ directory writes an extensionless binary
# named after that directory — a 9.6 MB one was committed this way. bin/ is the
# sanctioned output.
#
# gitignore cannot express "a file named after its parent directory" (no
# backreferences), and an un-anchored `define` would ignore the cmd/define/
# SOURCE directory, so the line below is necessarily per-tool: it only keeps
# `git status` quiet for the tools that exist. A new cmd/foo's binary will show
# up as untracked until someone adds a line.
#
# What covers the general case is the pair of guards in repo_guard_test.go, and
# each covers a different place:
#   TestNoCommittedBinaries  reads the INDEX   (git ls-files)
#   TestNoBinariesInHistory  reads the HISTORY (git rev-list --objects HEAD)
# The distinction is not academic. A binary removed in a follow-up commit
# leaves the index clean and the blob reachable — every clone still fetches it.
# Both were verified by planting a binary in a new cmd/newtool/: the index guard
# fails when it is staged, and after a git rm in a later commit the index guard
# passes while the history guard fails.
/cmd/*/define
